  "prediction": "The President's budget for FY2020, released in two installments in March 2019, consists of a multi-volume set of materials issued by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB). The budget documents include information on new proposals, summary tables, economic analysis, and supporting documents. The President is required to submit the budget to Congress by the first Monday in February, but incoming administrations typically do not release the multivolume budget sets until later in the year. Other related budget documents include the annual Economic Report of the President, issued by the Council of Economic Advisers, and the budget reports from the Congressional Budget Office.\n\nThe main budget document, the annual budget volume, contains information on the President's new budget proposals and summaries of government activities. Summary tables provide projections of budget baselines, revenues, and spending. The Major Savings and Reforms document outlines the Administration's proposed cost savings and reforms.\n\nAdditional budget documents include the FY2020 Budget in Brief, which provides a concise overview of the President's budget proposals, and the Historical Tables, which provide historical data on federal spending and revenues. The budget documents are available online from both OMB and the Government Publishing Office (GPO).\n\nPrinted copies of the budget documents can be purchased from GPO, and the CRS (Congressional Research Service) provides web pages covering the budget and appropriations process for Members of Congress and the public. The public can also access Congress.gov, the legislative website produced by the Library of Congress, for information on the status of appropriations legislation.",
  "scored_prediction": "The President's budget for FY2020, released in two installments in March 2019, consists of a multi-volume set of materials issued by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB). The budget documents include information on new proposals, summary tables, economic analysis, and supporting documents. The President is required to submit the budget to Congress by the first Monday in February, but incoming administrations typically do not release the multivolume budget sets until later in the year. Other related budget documents include the annual Economic Report of the President, issued by the Council of Economic Advisers, and the budget reports from the Congressional Budget Office.\n\nThe main budget document, the annual budget volume, contains information on the President's new budget proposals and summaries of government activities. Summary tables provide projections of budget baselines, revenues, and spending. The Major Savings and Reforms document outlines the Administration's proposed cost savings and reforms.\n\nAdditional budget documents include the FY2020 Budget in Brief, which provides a concise overview of the President's budget proposals, and the Historical Tables, which provide historical data on federal spending and revenues. The budget documents are available online from both OMB and the Government Publishing Office (GPO).\n\nPrinted copies of the budget documents can be purchased from GPO, and the CRS (Congressional Research Service) provides web pages covering the budget and appropriations process for Members of Congress and the public. The public can also access Congress.gov, the legislative website produced by the Library of Congress, for information on the status of appropriations legislation.",
  "score": 29.48207122077428,
  "answers": [
    "Every year the President submits a series of volumes to Congress containing the President's proposed budget for the coming fiscal year. The President's submission is required on or after the first Monday in January, but no later than the first Monday in February (31 U.S.C. \u00a71105(a)). This year the President released the budget submission, in two installments, on March 11, and March 18, 2019. This report provides brief descriptions of the FY2020 budget volumes and related documents, together with internet addresses, Government Publishing Office (GPO) stock numbers, and prices for obtaining print copies of these publications. It also explains how to find the locations of government depository libraries, which can provide both printed copies for reference use and internet access to the online versions. This report will be updated as events warrant. Please note that neither the Congressional Research Service (CRS) nor the Library of Congress (LOC) distributes print copies of the budget documents."
  ],
